SUMMARY:Ghosts of the American Dream
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260721T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260721T200000
UID:7fb874bfed4af87fab5400957b1ec327@littlejumbobar.com
DESCRIPTION:The American dream was a song long before it was anything you 
 could hold. It lived in parlor tunes and field hollers\, in protest verses
  and Sunday hymns\, in the things the country sang to itself to keep belie
 ving. Ghosts of the American Dream moves through that inheritance like a s
 low walk through rooms no one lives in anymore. A Stephen Foster lament ab
 out hard times sits beside Woody Guthrie's argument over who the land real
 ly belongs to\; Sister Rosetta Tharpe's vow to study war no more leans aga
 inst the breadline weariness of "Brother\, Can You Spare a Dime?" These ar
 e not covers so much as visitations.\n\nThe band is built for this kind of
  conjuring. Jay Sanders\, equally at home in the bass chair of Acoustic Sy
 ndicate and in the improvised guitar music he plays most weeks in this roo
 ...
LOCATION:Little Jumbo: 241 Broadway St.\, Asheville\, NC 28801
URL:https://www.littlejumbobar.com/events/1909/ghosts-of-the-american-drea
 m
